WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.250
The WHERE clause decides which rows remain in the
result This is

00:00:04.275 --> 00:00:07.725
one of the most important ideas in SQL because most
real questions

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:12.362
apply some kind of condition For simple comparisons
we can use equals

00:00:12.525 --> 00:00:16.188
not equal greater than greater than or equal less
than and less

00:00:16.212 --> 00:00:20.638
than or equal For example age greater than or equal
to sixty-five

00:00:20.662 --> 00:00:24.875
keeps patients who are at least sixty-five BETWEEN
is useful for a

00:00:24.888 --> 00:00:30.000
range Age BETWEEN thirty AND sixty includes both
endpoints so ages thirty

00:00:30.025 --> 00:00:34.238
and sixty are included IN checks whether a value
belongs to a

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:40.175
list For example a status can be either Admitted or
Discharged Instead

00:00:40.188 --> 00:00:43.762
of writing two separate equality conditions joined
by OR IN can make

00:00:43.788 --> 00:00:47.775
the query easier to read LIKE and ILIKE are used for
text

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:53.725
patterns In PostgreSQL ILIKE performs
case-insensitive matching which means uppercase

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:58.312
and lowercase letters do not have to match exactly
Missing values need

00:00:58.338 --> 00:01:02.700
special treatment We use IS NULL or IS NOT NULL
rather than

00:01:02.725 --> 00:01:08.112
ordinary equality Finally conditions can be combined
with AND OR and NOT

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.738
AND requires all connected conditions to be true OR
requires at least

00:01:13.762 --> 00:01:19.288
one When conditions become more complicated
parentheses help make the intended logic

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:19.738
clear
